From 7177aa125e6c253f31701aaa39df6ea49602f395 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gaia <build@heygaia.io>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 16:52:53 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] fix(js): a page's timer wakes reach whichever task polls it
 now

deno_core's timer queue wakes the waker of the last event-loop poll that
found no timer due; a poll that finds one due fires it and keeps no waker.
It assumes one task polls a runtime for life. The CDP server polls a page
from two: the navigation runs in a task of its own, then the connection
task pumps the page between commands. Page scripts also queue timers
before the event loop's first poll. So when a timer was already due at the
pump's first poll, that poll fired it, and the timer its callback queued
woke nobody (or the finished navigation task): the page froze until the
next CDP command arrived. bing.com's US homepage builds its search box,
buttons and links from a timer chain started by onload, and stayed blank;
the compat probe saw 0 inputs, buttons and links there on about half of
its CI runs.

deno_core is now always polled with one waker per page that forwards to
the task polling right now, and a new page polls once before any script
can queue a timer, so the timer queue holds that waker from the start.

Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Tm2SNEJ42hMPiZ6cFb8Vih
